{
  "core_question": "What analyses should a hospital board conduct when evaluating a private-equity acquisition involving conversion to for-profit, sale‑leaseback of real estate, and charitable foundation spin‑out?",
  "rationale": "The scenario spans clinical operations, legal compliance (nonprofit status, tax law, lease agreements), and financial feasibility; each domain requires specialized expertise.",
  "routes": ["healthcare", "legal", "finance"],
  "sub_questions": {
    "healthcare": "What clinical operational risks and patient care continuity issues arise when converting a nonprofit community hospital to a for‑profit entity under a sale‑leaseback arrangement, and how should the board assess impact on quality metrics, staffing, accreditation, and community health outcomes?",
    "legal": "What are the key legal considerations—including IRS rules on nonprofit conversion, charitable foundation structuring, real estate leaseback compliance, state hospital licensing, and regulatory approvals—when a private equity sponsor acquires a 90‑bed rural hospital and converts it to for-profit while spinning out proceeds into a charitable foundation?",
    "finance": "How should the board evaluate the financial feasibility of the acquisition, including valuation of the operating entity, cost and terms of the sale‑leaseback transaction, impact on debt capacity, cash flow projections, tax implications, and return expectations for both the private equity sponsor and the charitable foundation?"
  },
  "requires_recency_disclosure": true,
  "recency_notes": "Recent IRS guidance on nonprofit conversions (2023/24), updated real estate lease‑back tax rules, and evolving state hospital licensing requirements may affect analysis."
}
